1. The rule (tools/world)
- contracts/world/civic-look.json beds.level, written by `bun tools/world/civic/bed-level-import.ts --write` (never by hand). It's keyed by the bed's export id. For each of the five beds it holds the piece (CivicBedPlanting_<bed>, Vela's final ids) and the bed's outline in that piece's own plan frame. It also holds soilProud 0.003 and tol 0.001 m. Sources and sha256: the testbed's plaza-garden.bin.gz key outlines, Vela's domes077.manifest.json (civicBeds, finals.place) and out/decokit-077/decokit_manifest.json.
- tools/world/civic/beds.ts: fitRigid fits the outline onto the bed ring, vertex i to vertex i (2D Kabsch). bedLevelOf, and dressBeds(masses, level). A bed whose piece is adopted and whose ring fits within tol gets exactly one mass `<bed>/planting`:
  kit { id, yaw, at } = the fit, base = kerb top + 0.003 (KitPlacer puts the lowest point, the soil, at base). No hedge and no tufts. Its ring is the bed's ring and its height is the piece's height.
  If the piece isn't adopted, or the ring no longer fits, the bed keeps today's kerb, hedge and tufts, so no planter is ever empty. The rule has no seed, so it's deterministic.
- The start vertex is never searched. Three of the five beds are symmetric under a half turn, so a searched start fits them two ways, 0.01 mm apart, and can turn the composition 180°. That happened in Vela's 077 fit for facade-southwest: her k=13 fit, angle 0 against the true pi. The rule corrects it: it places that piece at yaw pi and the other four at yaw 0. Each placement lands as the testbed's bed moved whole.
- CD-5 (tools/world/rules/civic-dress.ts): every named bed must fit its outline. A planted bed must have its one piece and no hedge or tufts. A bed whose piece isn't adopted yet passes, and is listed in the detail as waiting. It runs only when the look has beds.level, so recorded runs' pinned looks are unaffected.

